{title:Title}

{phang}
{bf:xtboundpanico} {hline 2} Bounds and PANICO testing for cointegration in panels
with unobserved common factors


{marker syntax}{...}
{title:Syntax}

{p 8 17 2}
{cmd:xtboundpanico} {it:subcommand} {it:depvar} {it:indepvars}
{ifin} [{cmd:,} {it:options}]

{synoptset 24 tabbed}{...}
{synopthdr:subcommand}
{synoptline}
{synopt:{helpb xtboundpanico_bounds:bounds}}panel cointegration bounds test
(Section 3 of the paper){p_end}
{synopt:{helpb xtboundpanico_panico:panico}}PANICO with principal-component
factors (Section 4){p_end}
{synopt:{helpb xtboundpanico_panico:panicca}}PANICO with cross-section-average
factors{p_end}
{synopt:{helpb xtboundpanico_nfactors:nfactors}}number of common factors, every
criterion side by side{p_end}
{synopt:{cmd:all}}{cmd:bounds}, then {cmd:panico}, then {cmd:panicca}, then a
synthesis{p_end}
{synoptline}

{pstd}
The panel must be {helpb xtset} and strongly balanced with no gaps.
{it:depvar} is {it:y_it}; {it:indepvars} are the {it:k} weakly exogenous
stochastic regressors {it:x_it}.


{marker description}{...}
{title:Description}

{pstd}
{cmd:xtboundpanico} tests the null hypothesis of a {bf:panel spurious
relationship} against the alternative of {bf:panel cointegration} when the
cross-section dependence is generated by unobserved common factors whose
number and order of integration are unknown. It implements Banerjee and
Carrion-i-Silvestre (2026).

{pstd}
The data generating process is

        Y_it = D_it + L_i F_t + U_it,    (1-L) F_t = v_t,    (1-L) U_it = e_it

{pstd}
with Y_it = (y_it, x_it')'. The long-run slope vector {it:theta} is
{bf:homogeneous} across units; the speed of adjustment, the factor loadings
and the short-run dynamics are all heterogeneous. Under the null the
idiosyncratic component U_it is I(1), so no stationary combination of Y_it
exists.

{pstd}
The command offers the paper's two complementary answers to the same question.

{phang2}
{bf:1. Bounds testing} ({cmd:bounds}). The factors are proxied by cross-section
averages and {it:theta} is estimated by the pooled CCE estimator, which is
consistent even under the null. The panel statistic {it:tybar} is then compared
with {bf:two} critical values: one computed as if every factor were I(0) and one
as if every factor were I(1). If {it:tybar} falls outside that interval the
conclusion is robust to the number and order of integration of the factors. If
it falls inside, inference is {bf:inconclusive} and you move to PANICO.

{phang2}
{bf:2. PANICO} ({cmd:panico}, {cmd:panicca}). PANIC analysis of cointegration.
The differenced, projected series are defactored, the idiosyncratic component
and the factors are recumulated, and six statistics (P, Pm, Z, Pa, Pb, PMSB)
test the idiosyncratic component while the ADF or the MQ statistics pin down how
many factors are I(1). There is no ambiguity region, but the number of factors
must be estimated.

{pstd}
Every step is mapped to its equation in the paper in
{helpb xtboundpanico_methods:help xtboundpanico methods}.


{marker subcommands}{...}
{title:Subcommands}

{dlgtab:bounds}
{pstd}
Pooled CCE coefficients with Holly-Pesaran-Yamagata standard errors, the panel
bounds statistic {it:tybar}, both polar critical values at the 1, 2.5, 5 and 10%
levels, a verdict (cointegration / no cointegration / inconclusive), and the
CIPS comparator of Banerjee and Carrion-i-Silvestre (2017).
See {helpb xtboundpanico_bounds}.

{dlgtab:panico and panicca}
{pstd}
The six idiosyncratic statistics with p-values, plus the ADF test (one factor)
or the sequential MQc/MQf tests (more than one factor) on the estimated common
factors. {cmd:panico} extracts the factors by principal components;
{cmd:panicca} replaces them by differenced cross-section averages, as in Reese
and Westerlund (2016). See {helpb xtboundpanico_panico}.

{dlgtab:nfactors}
{pstd}
Eleven estimators of the number of common factors side by side: ICp1-ICp3,
PCp1-PCp3, AIC3 and BIC3 (Bai and Ng 2002), ER and GR (Ahn and Horenstein
2013) and ED (Onatski 2010). See {helpb xtboundpanico_nfactors}.

{dlgtab:all}
{pstd}
Runs the three testing subcommands in order and closes with the trade-off
discussed in Section 5.3 of the paper: the bounds test is simple and agnostic
but has an inconclusive region; PANICO removes the ambiguity at the price of
estimating {it:r}.


{marker options}{...}
{title:Options}

{pstd}
Options available to every subcommand:

{phang}
{opt mod:el(#)} sets the deterministic component: {cmd:0} none, {cmd:1} a
constant (default), {cmd:2} a constant and a linear trend. Because attention is
restricted to {it:rho_yi}, the paper's Cases 2 and 3 collapse into Model 1 and
Cases 4 and 5 into Model 2.

{phang}
{opt fac:tors(#)} fixes the number of common factors. For {cmd:bounds} the
default is {it:1+k}, the rank condition met with equality, and {it:#} may not
exceed {it:1+k}. For {cmd:panico} the default is to estimate it.

{phang}
{opt maxl:ags(#)} maximum VAR order. The default is the paper's
{it:floor(4 (T/100)^(1/4))}.

{phang}
{opt lags(#)} imposes a fixed order instead of selecting one.

{phang}
{opt lagc:rit(crit)} selection criterion: {cmd:bic} (default, as in the paper),
{cmd:aic}, {cmd:maic} or {cmd:mbic} (Ng and Perron 2001; Perron and Qu 2007).

{phang}
{opt lev:el(#)} confidence level for the printed verdict; the default is
{cmd:level(95)}. Only 90, 95, 97.5 and 99 map onto a tabulated critical value.

{phang}
{opt graph} draws a diagnostic figure; {opt name(name)} names it.

{pstd}
Subcommand-specific options are documented on each subcommand's help page.


{marker interpret}{...}
{title:Interpreting the output}

{pstd}
{bf:Pooled CCE long-run coefficients.} The estimate of the homogeneous slope
vector {it:theta}, consistent at rate min(sqrt(T), sqrt(N)) whether or not the
panel cointegrates. The standard error is the Holly, Pesaran and Yamagata
(2010) sandwich, so you can test a theory restriction such as
{it:theta = 1} directly from the reported z.

{pstd}
{bf:tybar.} The average over units of the t-ratio of the speed-of-adjustment
coefficient in the conditional error-correction model. Large negative values are
evidence of cointegration.

{pstd}
{bf:Lower / upper bound.} The lower bound assumes every factor is I(0); the
upper bound assumes every factor is I(1). The upper bound is the more negative
of the two, so

{p 8 12 2}{it:tybar} < upper bound{col 44}=> cointegration, robustly{p_end}
{p 8 12 2}{it:tybar} > lower bound{col 44}=> no cointegration, robustly{p_end}
{p 8 12 2}in between{col 44}=> inconclusive; run {cmd:panico}{p_end}

{pstd}
{bf:P, Pm, Z} are group-type: they pool the per-unit ADF statistics and tolerate
heterogeneous dynamics. {bf:Pa, Pb, PMSB} are pooled-type: they impose a common
autoregressive parameter and are more powerful when that is true. P is
chi2(2N) and is read in the upper tail; Pm in the upper tail of the standard
normal; Z, Pa, Pb and PMSB in the lower tail.

{pstd}
{bf:ADF on Fhat} (one factor) or {bf:MQc / MQf} (several) report how many common
factors carry a stochastic trend. That is exactly the information the bounds test
refuses to use, and it is what resolves an inconclusive bounds result.


{marker remarks}{...}
{title:Remarks and practical guidance}

{pstd}
{bf:Sample-size regime.} The asymptotics are joint, with sqrt(T)/N -> 0 for the
bounds test and N/T -> 0 for the PANICO statistics. In practice: the bounds test
is usable from about T = 50, but its ambiguity region is wide there (the paper
measures 0.28 probability mass at T = 50, N = 50 when the factor is I(1)) and
narrows quickly in T. PANICO is more powerful than the bounds test for
T >= 100 regardless of N.

{pstd}
{bf:Which statistic to trust.} The group-type Z, P and Pm over-reject mildly in
small T because asymptotic moments and p-values are used; the paper documents
this in Section 5.2. Pb and PMSB are the best behaved. Prefer them when T is
small, and read agreement across the six as the real evidence.

{pstd}
{bf:The rank condition.} With cross-section averages as factor proxies,
Assumption G caps {it:r} at {it:1+k}: only {it:1+k} averages exist. If you
believe there are more factors than that, use {cmd:panico}, which estimates
them by principal components and does not need the rank condition at all.

{pstd}
{bf:Choosing r.} Over-estimation is common when N or T is small. Run
{cmd:xtboundpanico nfactors} first and compare BIC3, ER, GR and ED before
settling: in the paper's own applications every Bai-Ng criterion selected
{it:rmax} while Onatski's ED and Ahn-Horenstein's ratios selected 1.
Over-specifying {it:r} leaves {cmd:bounds} with the I(1) critical value nearly
unaffected but makes the I(0) one over-reject more.

{pstd}
{bf:Slope homogeneity is assumed, not tested.} Equation (10) of the paper
imposes a common {it:theta}. That is the point of the method - it is what makes
{it:theta} consistently estimable under the null and gives the test its power
against theory restrictions such as purchasing power parity or the full Fisher
effect - but it is a restriction. If you need heterogeneous slopes, the
Gengenbach, Urbain and Westerlund (2016) framework is the right one, and this
command is not a substitute for it. No homogeneity test is provided because the
paper provides none.

{pstd}
{bf:What this command does not do.} The paper reports but does not tabulate
critical values for the Wald and F joint-significance variants of the individual
test, so they are not computed. There is no confidence interval for the speed of
adjustment. Critical values for the CIPS comparator are not tabulated here;
take them from Banerjee and Carrion-i-Silvestre (2017) or Pesaran, Smith and
Yamagata (2013).

{pstd}
{bf:There is no postestimation mode}, and this is deliberate. Every statistic
here is computed from the {bf:levels} of y_it and x_it - the cross-section
averages, the pooled CCE projection and the principal components all need the
raw panel - so there is nothing useful to recover from a fitted {helpb xtreg}
or {helpb regress} in memory. Like {helpb xtunitroot}, the command takes its
variables directly. It also leaves {cmd:e()} untouched, so you can run it
between estimation commands without disturbing them.

{pstd}
{bf:Balance.} Both the cross-section averages and the principal components need
a strongly balanced panel with no gaps and no missing values on any model
variable. The command errors out rather than silently dropping units; use
{helpb xtdescribe} and {helpb misstable} to find the problem.

{pstd}
{bf:Degrees of freedom.} Equation (17) of the paper writes the error variance
with a full {it:T} in the denominator. Taken literally that roughly doubles the
empirical size at T = 50 relative to the paper's own Table C.3, so the default
here is the ordinary OLS denominator. {opt dffull} and {opt dfpaper} expose the
two literal readings. The arithmetic and the Monte Carlo evidence are in
{helpb xtboundpanico_methods:help xtboundpanico methods}.


{marker examples}{...}
{title:Examples}

{pstd}Setup{p_end}
{phang2}{cmd:. webuse grunfeld}{p_end}
{phang2}{cmd:. xtset company year}{p_end}

{pstd}How many common factors?{p_end}
{phang2}{cmd:. xtboundpanico nfactors invest mvalue kstock, model(1) rmax(3)}{p_end}

{pstd}The bounds test, with a trend and both polar critical values{p_end}
{phang2}{cmd:. xtboundpanico bounds invest mvalue kstock, model(2)}{p_end}

{pstd}Resolve an inconclusive result with PANICO{p_end}
{phang2}{cmd:. xtboundpanico panico invest mvalue kstock, model(2) nfactors(bic3)}{p_end}

{pstd}The cross-section-average variant, with a diagnostic figure{p_end}
{phang2}{cmd:. xtboundpanico panicca invest mvalue kstock, model(2) graph}{p_end}

{pstd}Everything, with the synthesis{p_end}
{phang2}{cmd:. xtboundpanico all invest mvalue kstock, model(2)}{p_end}

{pstd}
A complete, self-checking demonstration on the paper's own Monte Carlo design -
including the replication of its Table C.3 - ships with the package as
{bf:xtboundpanico_example.do}. Retrieve it with
{bf:. net get xtboundpanico}.


